{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And they killed every male, et Hamor, etc.</b> It is difficult: why did they kill those who had not sinned? And further, why did they not start with the perpetrator first?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the sons of Jacob did not intend to kill anyone but the perpetrator; rather, all the men of the city wanted to stand in the breach against them to prevent them from killing their king — therefore they killed them by the law of the pursuer. And that is what the verse says: <b>and they killed every male</b>; and through this they managed to kill et Hamor and et Shechem. And without this, they would not have been able to exact vengeance from the one who was liable to them for death." "<b>Further,</b> a reason why they killed all the men of the city is because they had been Shechem's accomplices in abducting Dinah, and the sons of Noah are liable to death for robbery; but for illicit relations there is no liability, since Dinah was not a married woman." "<b>And you will find</b> that Scripture says ״אֲשֶׁר טִמְּאוּ אֲחוֹתָם״ (Genesis 34:26) — behold, it attributed the act to all of them. From this you learn that the hand of all of them was equal in the robbery. And the reason it did not say ״אֲשֶׁר גָּזְלוּ אֲחוֹתָם״ — which is the principal offense for which they were liable to death — is to say that this was a robbery that cannot be restored. And the implication is: had it not been for the reason of ״אֲשֶׁר טִמְּאוּ״ they would not have carried out a full judgment to kill every male, for even though a son of Noah who robbed is not exempt from death by returning the stolen property, with all this the sons of Jacob would not have killed them. For many among the nations transgress the commandments, and their accountability does not fall upon the sons of Jacob, for they have no power over them — as Jacob said (Genesis 34:30): ״עֲכַרְתֶּם אוֹתִי וְגוֹ׳ וַאֲנִי מְתֵי מִסְפָּר״. But because of the outrage they committed in defiling her, therefore they donned zealotry." "<b>Further,</b> a reason for its saying <b>asher tim'u</b> ('who defiled') — they gave this as the reason for taking their property, and it states the reason: because they plundered the city and took their possessions — that they defiled Dinah their sister. Meaning, payment for shame, for according to the measure of the one who shames and the one who is shamed — since these are vastly differing degrees — would that their entire wealth suffice for the shame-payment of Jacob's daughter, who was unique in the world at the summit of ranks, compared with Hamor who was exceedingly lowly. And also according to what I wrote above, that he confessed to the act — this helps only to exempt him from the fine, but for shame and other matters he is liable (Ketuvot 41a), and this is straightforward." "<b>And the Rambam</b> in chapter 9 of the Laws of Kings (Hilkhot Melakhim 9:14) gave a reason for the killing of the men of Shechem — because they did not appoint judges for the robbery of Shechem, etc. — end quote. And it is difficult: who told the Master that they are obligated to judge the mute matter concerning them — a king and the like — when even under the laws of Israel they are exempt from such a case — they do not judge and do not testify concerning him (Sanhedrin 16a). And perforce one must say that they were his helpers and by their counsel he did what he did. And the Ramban, may his memory be for a blessing, rejected the words of the Rambam in the commandment of judges with which the children of Noah were commanded, for the intent is not to appoint judges but rather to judge matters between a man and his fellow — one should examine his words there. And he gave a reason for the killing of the men of Shechem — because they were liable to death for being idol worshippers, etc. And his words, may his memory be for a blessing, require examination. And the two luminaries did not give a reason for the taking of their property and their wealth."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {5 "The phrase 'האלם עליהם' (literally 'the mute matter upon them') is obscure; rendered hyper-literally. The reference appears to concern the legal inaction or silence of the city regarding Shechem's crime, possibly echoing the rabbinic concept of an 'ir ha-niddaḥat' (city led astray) where silent complicity is at issue. The two luminaries (שני המאורות) refers to Rambam and Ramban."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:46:15.885767911Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}